<!--
================================================================================
  FOOTBALL MANAGER - EDITOR DATA (human-readable source file)
================================================================================

  WHAT THIS IS
  A complete, well-formed blueprint of the data changes you want to make in
  Football Manager: clubs, players, attributes, contracts and transfers. It
  uses FM's real data model so it maps 1:1 onto the in-game editor's fields.

  IMPORTANT: HOW FM ACTUALLY STORES EDITS
  The official FM in-game / pre-game editor saves its projects as a COMPILED
  BINARY ".fmf" file. There is no supported way to hand-write a .fmf, and the
  editor has no "import arbitrary XML data" button. So this XML is NOT loaded
  directly by the game. Use it as the authoritative spec:

    1. Open the Football Manager Editor (Steam > Library > Tools).
    2. File > New Database (pick the same data level you ship for).
    3. Recreate each <club>/<player>/<transfer> below using these exact values.
    4. File > Save (or Build) -> produces "your-edits.fmf".
    5. Drop the .fmf in:
         Documents/Sports Interactive/Football Manager 20XX/editor data/
    6. Tick it on the "Add/Remove" screen when starting a new save.

  CONVENTIONS USED BELOW
  * Attributes:        integer 1-20 (FM player attribute scale).
  * current_ability /
    potential_ability: integer 1-200 (FM CA/PA scale). For PA, a NEGATIVE value
                       means a random range, mirroring the editor:
                         -10 = 170-200, -9 = 150-180, -8 = 130-160, -75 = exactly 75+random.
  * Positions:         rating 1-20 for how natural a player is there
                       (20 = natural, 1 = makeshift). Omit a position to leave it 1.
  * Dates:             YYYY-MM-DD.
  * Money:             whole units of the club's currency (no separators).
  * unique_id:         editor entity id. Use negative ids for NEW entities you
                       are creating so they never clash with the base database.
                       Reference real existing entities by their real positive id.
================================================================================
-->
